Creating a script to protect sensitive information on a website involves a combination of server-side and client-side security measures. Below is a simplified example using JavaScript (client-side) and a backend language like Node.js (Express) for server-side protection. This script focuses on basic input validation, encryption, and secure data handling.

**1. Server-Side (Node.js with Express)**

First, set up a basic Express server to handle requests and protect routes.

```javascript
// server.js
const express = require('express');
const bodyParser = require('body-parser');
const crypto = require('crypto');

const app = express();
const port = 3000;

app.use(bodyParser.json());

// In-memory data storage (for demonstration)
let sensitiveData = {};

// Protected route to handle sensitive data
app.post('/submit-data', (req, res) => {
    const { name, email, password } = req.body;

    // Basic input validation
    if (!name || !email || !password) {
        return res.status(400).json({ error: 'All fields are required' });
    }

    // Encrypt sensitive data
    const encryptedPassword = crypto.createHash('sha256').update(password).digest('hex');
    sensitiveData[email] = encryptedPassword;

    res.status(200).json({ message: 'Data submitted successfully' });
});

app.listen(port, () => {
    console.log(`Server running on http://localhost:${port}`);
});
```

